-- **Shiki,** with one of two engines, by one rule wherever a page renders ([WebAssembly in the frames](https://github.com/comp-markdown/compmd/blob/main/docs/webassembly-in-the-frames.md), decision 2): Oniguruma, as WebAssembly, where the page compiles it (the frames' policy allows it; a host-mode page's allows it with `'wasm-unsafe-eval'`, which `@compmd/vite`'s `cspFor` gives), except in Chromium; its JavaScript regex engine in Chromium (told by `navigator.userAgentData`) and in a page that refuses WebAssembly. In WebKit the JavaScript engine compiles each regex the first time it runs, which blocks the page for about a second on a first TypeScript block, where Oniguruma takes 60 ms; in Chromium the JavaScript engine is as fast, and Oniguruma would cost each frame 85 ms more ([the spike](https://github.com/comp-markdown/compmd/blob/main/spikes/highlight-perf/README.md)). The JavaScript engine leaves out a grammar's patterns it can't translate, so a block may be highlighted a little differently in Chromium. A host that gives `shikiHighlight` itself chooses with `shikiHighlight({ webAssembly: true })`. Its core and engine load the first time a page has a code block; each language's grammar the first time a block of it shows. Until then, and for a block it can't highlight, the text shows as it is. A block is highlighted again only when its text changes. The highlighter gives each line's tokens, and `@compmd/render` draws them into the line's element, which is its own, so a line's height (`view.lineHeights`) holds before and after the tokens come.
